description:
PERF (found by s540d while doing #4983, 2026-11-15; the brief asked for every per-print table read to be listed): once ANY filehandle is tied, every print to ANY handle -- tied or not -- probes the weak EQ table **p-handle-tie-table** (cl/pcl-runtime.lisp: %p-stream-tie / %p-fh-tie, one fixnum test while none is tied, then a GETHASH on a weak table = a synchronized one in SBCL, ~57 ns).  print does it TWICE: once where the designator is resolved (the `(if (%p-stream-tie s) s (%p-writable-stream s))` arm near %p-write-list's caller) and once at %p-write-list's entry.

MEASURED (heavy.sh bench, quiet, ab.pl best of 7 whole runs, base-vs-base band <= 0.9 %; scratch/s540d/legs/bench3.log in the s540d worktree): `open my $fh, ">", "/dev/null"; print $fh "x\n" for 1..1_000_000` takes 0.080 s; the same program with `tie *OTHER, 'TH'` first (a handle the loop never touches) takes 0.215 s on main 24988e35 (+169 %, ~130 ns a print) and 0.211 s on the s540d tree (+161 %) -- pre-existing, not moved by s540d.  perl: 0.04 s both ways.

REPRODUCER (rp/ftie.pl):
  use strict; use warnings;
  { package TH; sub TIEHANDLE { bless {}, shift } sub PRINT { 1 } }
  tie *OTHER, 'TH';
  open my $fh, ">", "/dev/null" or die; for my $i (1 .. 1_000_000) { print $fh "x\n" } close $fh; print "done\n";

THE LEVER: the shape #4983 shipped for $| (s540d: **p-autoflush-mirror**, a simple-vector of at most 8 weak pointers compared with EQ, re-taken when the table's count moves; the table stays the authority) -- a tie table holds few handles (Test::Builder / Capture-style code ties one or two).  And do the lookup ONCE per print (hand the record from the resolution to %p-write-list).  Bar: the tied-OTHER program back inside the band of the untied one; tie-handle rows of Pl/t + perl-tests tie_fh*.t + the companion op/tiehandle.t unchanged.  Who ties a handle in practice: test harnesses capturing output, IO::Scalar-style code, Tie::Handle subclasses -- every later print in such a program pays it.

NOT: a per-handle flag on the stream (a CL stream has no slot; the s514d design keyed on the resolved stream on purpose, docs/tie-aggregates.md Â§7).
